  "prediction": "**Department of Defense's Efforts to Prevent and Respond to Unwanted Sexual Behaviors**\n\nThe Department of Defense (DOD) has defined various types of unwanted sexual behaviors, including sexual assault, sexual harassment, and domestic violence. The report highlights the DOD's efforts to prevent and respond to these behaviors, but also identifies areas for improvement.\n\n**Current Policies and Procedures**\n\nThe DOD has established policies and procedures to prevent and respond to unwanted sexual behaviors, including the development of a comprehensive sexual harassment policy. However, the report finds that some of these policies do not include key elements, such as anonymous reporting of incidents and principles from the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention's (CDC) framework for preventing sexual violence.\n\n**CDC Framework for Preventing Sexual Violence**\n\nThe CDC framework includes six principles for preventing sexual violence: risk factors, protective factors, primary strategies for prevention, secondary strategies for prevention, tertiary strategies for prevention, and risk domains. The report finds that the DOD's policies do not consistently include all of these principles.\n\n**Data Collection and Reporting**\n\nThe report also examines the DOD's data collection and reporting efforts for unwanted sexual behaviors. While the DOD has developed reliable data systems for collecting and reporting data on some of these behaviors, inconsistencies in data elements and definitions may be creating difficulties in developing department-wide reports.\n\n**Recommendations**\n\nThe report makes four recommendations to the DOD:\n\n1. Fully include the CDC principles in the new policy for sexual harassment.\n2. Include mechanisms for anonymous reporting of incidents in the new policy for sexual harassment.\n3. Develop standard data elements and definitions for maintaining and reporting information on sexual harassment incidents.\n4. Incorporate all the elements that are key for establishing a long-term, results-oriented strategic planning framework in the continuum of harm prevention strategy.\n\n**Conclusion**\n\nThe DOD has made progress in preventing and responding to unwanted sexual behaviors, but there is still work to be done. By implementing the recommendations outlined in this report, the DOD can improve its policies and procedures, enhance data collection and reporting, and ultimately reduce the incidence of unwanted sexual behaviors in the military.",
